<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">What is a uniaxial base?</h2>



<figure class=3D"alignwide"><table style=3D"background-color: #f5fcf5; bord=
er-left:5px solid #baeaba; font-size:small;"><tbody>
<tr><td style=3D"padding: 5px;">Post is a part of a larger series (Uniaxial=
 base):<br>
<ul>
<li><a href=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/traditional-origami-bases/">Tradi=
tional Origami Bases</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/uniaxial-base/">Uniaxial base</a>=
</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/universal-molecule/">Universal mo=
lecule</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/rabbit-ear-fold/">Rabbit ear</a><=
/li>
</ul>
</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p>Every origami model, no matter how complex, has a base in its core. Thos=
e bases can vary in their complexity, from simple traditional origami bases=
 to complex custom made bases that can be seen today.&nbsp;</p>



<p>Nevertheless, one class of origami bases stand out: a uniaxial base.</p>



<p><strong><strong>A uniaxial base is a class of origami bases in which, wh=
en collapsed all its flaps lay on a single axis, and its hinge creases are =
perpendicular to that axis. </strong></strong>That is its definition, but w=
hat does this definition mean?&nbsp;</p>



<p>I will try to explain, but before that, I would like to underline once a=
gain that uniaxial bases are not something unusual and rare. Quite contrary=
. These bases are ever-present.&nbsp;</p>



<p>You already come across such bases, since all four well known <a href=3D=
"https://abrashiorigami.com/traditional-origami-bases/">traditional origami=
 bases</a> are uniaxial. In figure 1, you can see one of them =E2=80=93 a t=
raditional fish base. It is fairly simple since it has only four flaps, and=
 all of them lay on the same axis.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding=3D"async" width=
=3D"1024" height=3D"498" src=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploa=
ds/2020/12/Slika-1.jpg" alt=3D"Frog base" class=3D"wp-image-784"><figcaptio=
n>Figure 1</figcaption></figure>



<p>Furthermore, axial creases are clearly visible on the crease pattern.&nb=
sp; You see, by definition, axial creases are lines that connect circles=E2=
=80=99 centers, thus forming two distinctive triangles (in figure 1, axial =
creases are marked in green). These triangles are known as axial polygons. =
Now, before we move on, it is important to emphasize that axial polygons do=
 not necessarily have to be triangles. Any convex shape will do, but triang=
les and quadrangles are the most common.&nbsp;</p>



<p>If you look at figure 1 once again, you will see that upon collapsing al=
l these green lines forms a single axis.</p>



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Nonuniaxial base</h2>



<p>Even though uniaxial bases are ever-present, that does not mean that all=
 bases are necessarily uniaxial. Quite contrary. For instance, very prolifi=
c origami artist <a href=3D"http://www.johnmontroll.com/">John Montroll</a>=
 has developed and subsequently used several non-uniaxial bases. A great ex=
ample of such a base is shown in figure 2. That base was developed by Montr=
oll while designing his dinosaur models (diagrams for those dinosaurs=E2=80=
=99 models were published in his book =E2=80=93 <a href=3D"https://www.amaz=
on.com/Prehistoric-Origami-Dinosaurs-Other-Creatures/dp/0486265889">Prehist=
oric Origami: Dinosaurs and Other Creatures</a>). As you can see in figure =
2, the base is obviously not uniaxial</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding=3D"async" width=
=3D"1024" height=3D"503" src=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploa=
ds/2020/12/Slika-2.jpg" alt=3D"Nonuniaxial base" class=3D"wp-image-785"><fi=
gcaption>Figure 2</figcaption></figure>



<p>Why then, most of the people believe that uniaxial bases are the only on=
es. Well, first of all, the fact that almost all traditional origami bases =
are uniaxial could be a dominant reason. But there is something else. You s=
ee, there is a software such as <a href=3D"https://langorigami.com/article/=
treemaker/">TreeMaker </a>(developed by Robert Lang), that is based solely =
on the circle packing method and the uniaxial base theory. Those two facts =
led to the widespread belief that a circle packing method can produce only =
a uniaxial base and vice versa that the uniaxial bases could be made only b=
y using the circle packing method. I have to admit that this belief could s=
eem logical and accurate and that it has very sound foundations. However, t=
hat line of thinking is not entirely true. Uniaxial bases could be made usi=
ng other techniques as well. Namely, we can make one using the box plating =
method.</p>



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Box pleated uniaxial base</h2>



<p>Even though uniaxial bases are in most cases a result of a circle packin=
g technique it is surprisingly easy to construct box pleated uniaxial bases=
. One such base can be seen in figure 3.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding=3D"async" width=
=3D"1024" height=3D"502" src=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/wp-content/uploa=
ds/2020/12/Slika-5.jpg" alt=3D"Box pleated uniaxial base" class=3D"wp-image=
-786"><figcaption>Figure 3</figcaption></figure>



<p>There is no doubt that the base is uniaxial. You only have to flatten th=
e base and look at all flap=E2=80=99s tips. The fact that the base is uniax=
ial will become apparent immediately. Besides, all <a href=3D"https://abras=
hiorigami.com/hinge-crease/">hinge creases</a> are perpendicular to the pri=
me axis, which is also one of the preconditions for uniaxiality.&nbsp;&nbsp=
;</p>



<p>We could come to the same conclusion if we analyse the crease pattern on=
ly. At first glance, it is apparent that all circle centres are at the pape=
r edges. Meaning all of them are at elevation zero. That also means that th=
e flaps=E2=80=99 tips are at that same elevation.</p>



<p>On the other hand, if we design a base in which some of the circle cente=
rs are at different elevations, the uniaxiality of the base will be lost. I=
n figure 4, you can see such a base.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img loading=3D"lazy" decoding=
=3D"async" width=3D"1024" height=3D"508" src=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/=
wp-content/uploads/2020/12/Slika-6.jpg" alt=3D"Box pleated nonuniaxial base=
" class=3D"wp-image-787"><figcaption>Figure 4</figcaption></figure>



<p>As you can see one circle center is at different elevations consequently=
 making the base non-uniaxial.</p>



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Importance of uniaxial bases</h2>



<p>Origami is vast uncharted territory. Maybe such a statement is not somet=
hing that you would expect from me, but the truth has to be told. But, due =
to the work of <a href=3D"https://langorigami.com/">Robert Lang</a>, Takaha=
shi Meguro, and few other origami masters one segment of origami is =E2=80=
=9Ccharted=E2=80=9D to the point that software can be developed. For exampl=
e, an already mentioned TreeMaker, developed by Robert Lang. Obviously, to =
make the development of such software possible, a comprehensive theory has =
to exist.</p>



<h3 class=3D"wp-block-heading">The theory behind the uniaxial base</h3>



<p>The first step in the crease pattern design process, as you probably kno=
w, is to pack a needed number of circles and rivers on a square piece of pa=
per. The process itself is the most creative part of a base design even tho=
ugh it can be done automatically by software such as TreeMaker.</p>



<p>In figure 5, an example of one simple uniaxial base is given.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img loading=3D"lazy" decoding=
=3D"async" width=3D"1024" height=3D"908" src=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/=
wp-content/uploads/2020/12/Slika-3.jpg" alt=3D"Uniaxial base" class=3D"wp-i=
mage-789"><figcaption>Figure 5</figcaption></figure>



<p>The next step in the procedure asks for adding creases between centers o=
f all adjacent circles. These new lines, marked in green in figure 6, are a=
xial creases that form axial polygons.</p>



<p>The base in figure 5, is a nice example since the two most often used ty=
pes of axial polygons are present: triangles and quadrangles. Higher-order =
polygons are hardly ever used since they could always be reduced to triangl=
es and quadrangles by introducing an additional circle or flap.</p>



<p>Since that is supposed to be a uniaxial base, all these axial creases mu=
st coincide when the base is collapsed. But, to enable that, we have to add=
 additional creases in every single axial polygon. These additional creases=
 are most commonly known as molecules.</p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img loading=3D"lazy" decoding=
=3D"async" width=3D"1024" height=3D"496" src=3D"https://abrashiorigami.com/=
wp-content/uploads/2020/12/Slika-4.jpg" alt=3D"Molecules" class=3D"wp-image=
-790"><figcaption>Figure 6</figcaption></figure>



<h3 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Triangle molecule</h3>



<p>The simplest of all and most widely used type of molecule is the triangl=
e molecule. That molecule is used whenever we encounter axial polygons in t=
he shape of a triangle. Triangle molecule is so versatile that traditional =
origami bases (Kite, Fish, Bird, and Frog base) have nothing but triangles =
and triangle molecule known as rabbit ear. To form the rabbit ear, we only =
have to draw bisectors of all triangle=E2=80=99s angles. They will undoubte=
dly meet at a single point. Finally, we have to add a crease from that poin=
t perpendicular to one of the edges of the triangle. In figure 6. you can s=
ee how it is done.</p>



<h3 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Quadrilateral molecules</h3>



<p>Although triangles are the most common, they are not the only ones. Some=
times axial polygons are quadrangles. When we encounter such a polygon, we =
have to rely on more complex molecules. There are a handful of molecules th=
at can be used, like Waterbomb molecule, Arrowhead molecule, Sawhorse molec=
ule, etc. Some of them are specialized while some are more versatile, but t=
he most versatile of all is the so-called Gusset molecule. It is so versati=
le that all other molecules, except the Arrowhead molecule, are in fact its=
 special cases. An example of such a molecule is shown in figure 6.</p>



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Conclusion</h2>



<p>The uniaxial base is the most advanced origami concept developed so far.=
 Very few areas of origami are scrutinized as the Uniaxial base.&nbsp;</p>



<p>Even though uniaxial bases are quite versatile and as such widely used, =
they are not universally applicable. The technique is best suited for bugs =
with lots of legs and tentacles. Unfortunately, such a large number of flap=
s are not always needed, so the origami authors have to resort to some othe=
r techniques and ideas.</p>



<p>Nevertheless, studying uniaxial bases is a must for everyone who is stri=
ving toward origami mastery. Uniaxial bases can give you great insight into=
 the origami theory simply because they are well studied, documented, and e=
xplained.</p>
